      {
        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "df63cec0-25aa-5f66-be61-7583104da994",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- People: Stefan Edberg, Édouard Roger-Vasselin, Julien Benneteau, Brad Gilbert\n- Events: 1984 unofficial Olympic tournament, 1990 Thriftway ATP Championships – Singles, Roland Garros 2014\n- Topics: tennis achievements, rankings, playing style\n\nFacts:\n- Stefan Edberg won the unofficial Olympic tennis tournament in 1984.\n- Between 1985 and 1996, Stefan Edberg won six Grand Slam singles titles and three Grand Slam men's doubles titles.\n- In 1990, defending champion Brad Gilbert lost the 1990 Thriftway ATP Championships – Singles final to Stefan Edberg by 6–1, 6–1.\n- Stefan Edberg won the Masters Grand Prix, four Masters Series titles, and four Championship Series titles.\n- Stefan Edberg was part of four Swedish Davis Cup-winning teams.\n- Stefan Edberg, born on 19 January 1966, is a Swedish former professional tennis player who reached world No. 1 in both singles and doubles.\n- Stefan Edberg was a major proponent of the serve-and-volley style and stayed in the singles top 10 for ten consecutive years, including nine years in the top 5.\n- Stefan Edberg is considered one of the greatest tennis players of his era.\n- In 2014, Édouard Roger-Vasselin and Julien Benneteau won the men's doubles title at Roland Garros.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "0ef31b1f-9b66-5b5f-a8eb-5fed4294a8f6",
        "text": "Page: Stefan Edberg. Sentence: Stefan Bengt Edberg (] ; born 19 January 1966) is a Swedish former world no. 1 professional tennis player (in both singles and doubles).\nPage: Stefan Edberg. Sentence: A major proponent of the serve-and-volley style of tennis, he won six Grand Slam singles titles and three Grand Slam men's doubles titles between 1985 and 1996.\nPage: Stefan Edberg. Sentence: He also won the Masters Grand Prix and was a part of the Swedish Davis Cup-winning-team four times.\nPage: Stefan Edberg. Sentence: In addition he won four Masters Series titles, four Championship Series titles and the unofficial Olympic tournament 1984, was ranked in the singles top 10 for ten successive years, 9 years in the top 5, and is considered one of the greatest players of his era.\nPage: Édouard Roger-Vasselin. Sentence: He won the men's doubles title at Roland Garros in 2014, partnering Julien Benneteau.\nPage: 1990 Thriftway ATP Championships – Singles. Sentence: Brad Gilbert was the defending champion but Stefan Edberg defeated him 6–1, 6–1 in the final.",
        "type": "DocumentChunk"
